1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:06,590
A recorded model answer tells a customer their refund was processed. It
wasn't.

2
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:12,630
The runtime catches the false claim, sends the run to review, and zero refunds
move.

3
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:19,230
Yesterday, a real model's twenty-four case run produced four claims like this,
and none got through.

4
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:26,910
This is agent-runtime-evals: a refund agent with a human approval gate, on
synthetic data.

5
00:00:27,330 --> 00:00:33,520
The offline gate replays twenty-four recorded cases: twenty-four of twenty-
four pass.

6
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:38,000
A forced regression turns it red, with exit code one.

7
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:46,380
Now one live take: a real xAI model, three cases, and a hard cap of two cents.

8
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:56,250
Three of three pass, live. Ten calls, for about two-thirds of a cent.

9
00:00:56,340 --> 00:01:00,750
Small sample: three cases, one model, one run.

10
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:11,550
For a client, I'd phase it: shadow mode, then gated send for one refund
reason, then expand, with new eval cases first.

11
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:16,680
Risks: rubber-stamp approvers, and phrasings the detector doesn't know.

12
00:01:16,810 --> 00:01:20,660
The runbook covers roles, monitoring, and incidents.

13
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:28,990
The ROI is an estimate, [EST]: about two hundred ninety dollars a working day,
until a pilot validates it.

